// The pbrmaterial package: what goes into a physically based material, as
// opposed to how it is lit. The render package evaluates the lighting (the
// BRDF, the lights, the environment); this package says what the surface
// is: the parameters a metallic-roughness material takes, and, for now,
// textures of them baked from procedural noise. A program reaches it with
// 'import pbrmaterial', and gets:
//
// - Periodic fractal sums (fractal.cone): FractalLayer, fBm, ridged or
//   billowed gradient noise (the noise package's 'gradient2Periodic') whose
//   lattice wraps every 'period' cells, so a tile of it repeats with no
//   seam; an optional domain warp, periodic too.
// - Maps (maps.cone): ScalarMap, a square grid of f32 (a height field, a
//   mask) read with wrap-around, so a neighbourhood across an edge is the
//   texels on the opposite edge; TexelMap, 8-bit texels in the layout
//   render's Image keeps (rows bottom first, 3 or 4 bytes a texel), which
//   render uploads as a texture; 'mapHash', a 64-bit FNV-1a of a map's bytes,
//   what tests pin.
// - Per-texel functions (texel.cone), each a pure function of its
//   parameters (seeds among them), the texel's coordinates and, after the
//   first, the height map: the height ('heightTexel'); from the height map,
//   the tangent-space normal ('normalTexel'), convexity ('convexityTexel')
//   and ambient occlusion by horizon search ('occlusionTexel'); and the
//   surface: base colour, roughness, metallic, with edge wear and grime
//   masks ('surfaceTexel').
// - The bake (bake.cone): ProceduralSurface, every parameter of one
//   material, and 'bakeTextureSet', which runs the per-texel functions over
//   a square tile and gives a TextureSet: base colour (sRGB), an ORM map
//   (occlusion, roughness, metallic in red, green, blue, linear: glTF's
//   packing), a tangent-space normal map (linear, +y towards +v, glTF's
//   and OpenGL's convention), the height, and the wear and grime masks.
//
// HOW IT RUNS. Two passes, as a chain of GPU kernels would: the height of
// every texel first, then everything derived from the height map and the
// parameters, each texel independently. Every per-texel function is pure
// (no state, no allocation, no order), so each pass is the body of a
// kernel; today the bake runs them in a loop on the CPU. The whole package
// produces images, data whose size is chosen at run time: it is a tool
// package, not a maths one, though its per-texel functions return fixed-size
// values.
//
// DETERMINISM. Every random number is the noise package's integer hashing of
// (seed, lattice point); there is no stream and no platform RNG, so a bake is
// the same on every machine whose float operations round alike. Textures are
// for appearance only, so the derived maps use square roots and powers (the
// normal's length, the sRGB curve) freely.
//
// Coordinates: texel (x, y) is x from the left and y from the bottom of a
// tile 'size' texels square; its centre is at u = (x + 1/2) / size,
// v = (y + 1/2) / size, in tile units (the tile is 1 across). The height is
// 0 to 1, scaled by the surface's 'depth' (a fraction of the tile's width)
// into tile units wherever a slope is taken.
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports:
//
//   fractal.cone   FractalLayer: periodic fBm, ridged, billowed, warped
//   maps.cone      ScalarMap, TexelMap, encodings, mapHash
//   texel.cone     the parameters and the per-texel functions
//   bake.cone      ProceduralSurface, TextureSet, bakeTextureSet

mod pbrmaterial;

import libc;
import geomath use *;
import collections use *;
import noise use *;
